[{:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-35844"] :question/problem "What is the trace in the $yz$-plane of the hyperbolic paraboloid $z-3=\\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{5}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the trace in the $yz$-plane, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the paraboloid, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-3 & =\\frac{(0-5)^{2}}{5}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} \\\\ z-3 & =\\frac{25}{5}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} \\\\ z-3 & =5-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} \\\\ z-8 & =-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-4(z-8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives us the equation of the parabola in $yz$-plane." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-35927"] :question/problem "Given the elliptic paraboloid $z+4=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{4}$, find its intercepts with the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To find the $x$-intercepts of the paraboloid, we substitute $y=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation of the paraboloid and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+4 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(0-4)^{2}}{4} \\\\ 4 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{16}{4} \\\\ 4 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+4 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the solution $x=0$\n\nTherefore, the intercept with the $x$-axis is $(0,0,0)$ only." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-73864/selection/128e8f9908f4a8ebe3448318bc6e22744c72c5b20d279440757d9304a41011dc" :answer/id #uuid "465d0522-44cf-5983-b966-2ec47c1c7217" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(0,-3,8)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-73864/selection/ddeeaf6b0622b6a683d74d09dd7034d011c689d9e2b8df148e6fafbdd239ef6c" :answer/id #uuid "b1bb1124-5686-5d5d-98d4-948cb40ed59e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(0,-9,-8)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-73864/selection/42d61561cae06affe1fc4b17d7f8ad9cd3ab4cfa18d18375d27e558d819beca5" :answer/id #uuid "c8261ad0-2fe4-5a20-